; diag.tal - diagonal stripes, one colour, sixteen to the period.
;
;   ./duxasm -o roms/diag.rom asm/diag.tal
;   ./duxsdl roms/diag.rom
;
; (x + y) mod 16 under four. Skipping is therefore "four or more", which is
; $0003 and GTH2 rather than $0004 and LTH2 - getting that backwards leaves the
; eightieth of the screen that should be lit drawn in the background colour, and
; the total still comes to 16000. The count could not tell the difference; the
; histogram could, because the lit pixels were the background colour. The screen is 320 wide and sixteen divides 320
; exactly, so every row has exactly eighty lit pixels whatever y is, and the
; count is 80 * 200 = 16000 without having to render it to find out.
;
; The sum is a short and the mask is a short mask. Both counters here are
; shorts, so widening the sum to a byte would wrap at 256 - and the stripes
; would still come out right, because 256 is a multiple of sixteen. That is not
; a reason to do it: it is a reason to notice how easily a wrong answer looks
; like a right one.

INCLUDE "common.tal"

@draw
        JSR2 @clear
        LIT2 $0000
        STA2 $ZP.sj
@row
        LIT2 $0000
        STA2 $ZP.si
@col
        LDA2 $ZP.si
        LDA2 $ZP.sj
        ADD2
        LIT2 $000f
        AND2
        LIT2 $0003
        GTH2
        JNZ @col-end
        LIT $01
        STA $ZP.t
        LDA2 $ZP.si
        STA2 $ZP.rx
        LDA2 $ZP.sj
        STA2 $ZP.ry
        LDZ2 $ZP.rx
        LDZ2 $ZP.ry
        LDZ $ZP.t
        JSR2 @plot
@col-end
        LDA2 $ZP.si
        LIT2 $0001
        ADD2
        STA2 $ZP.si
        LDA2 $ZP.si
        LIT2 $0140
        GTH2
        JNZ @col-end2
        JMP @col
@col-end2
        LDA2 $ZP.sj
        LIT2 $0001
        ADD2
        STA2 $ZP.sj
        LDA2 $ZP.sj
        LIT2 $00c8
        GTH2
        JNZ @row-end
        JMP @row
@row-end
        JMP2r2
